# Financial knowledge integration: audit and implementation plan

Status: Batches 0-1 implemented on 2026-10-18, with the Section 8 correction and
cash-flow slice; the Section 9, 10 and 11 corrections and four Batch 2 slices
(return measurement and present value; descriptive statistics and probability;
statistical inference and simulation) completed the same day (Sections 9.4, 10.4
and 11.4). Python/uv/Make standardisation is recorded in Section 12. The follow-up
audit in Section 13 identified bounded content and run-command corrections and
three foundation regression objectives; both were implemented the same day
(Section 13.4), and a bounded correction pass on that regression slice followed
(Section 13.5), then a second bounded correction (Section 13.6) and the
portfolio moments and diversification slice (Section 14). The guide became a
committed copy (Section 15). The portfolio choice and asset pricing batch
(Section 16) completed the remaining `topic:portfolio-risk-return` objectives
and all eight `topic:asset-pricing-factor-models` objectives, including the two
analysis-level ones, after two audit corrections; a fixes-only correction
pass followed (Section 17), which also moved remote CI to merged pull requests.
Standing batch preference: each content-expansion prompt targets 45 newly
completed objectives in addition to any audit fixes (Section 17.4; raised from 30
on 2026-10-19, and the batches of Sections 18 and 19 keep their 30-objective records). The performance and planning batch (Section 18) then
covered 30 objectives in index construction, investment planning, behavioural finance, risk
governance, active management, performance evaluation, manager selection and GIPS, on a
branch stacked on PR #22. No next content slice has been selected. Not started: the remaining `topic:linear-regression` objectives
(multiple regression, misspecification, extensions); the rest of Batch 2; and
the rest of Batches 3-6. (Later batches are in Sections 20-29; the linear-regression objectives
named here were completed in Section 29.)
Current summary (2026-10-20): after the audit corrections of Section 32, the completion batch
of Section 33 and the final audit corrections of Section 34, all 424 objectives and all 1,030 outline items are covered (editorial coverage),
with professional ethics and private wealth management added as chapters. Remaining work is
human review of the draft entries and study; no content batch is pending.
Editorial coverage only: nothing here records or implies learner mastery.
Audit date: 2026-10-18, Asia/Singapore.
Repository baseline: `dc74b55`; pinned guide: `343d145909ba5384202b50604bf5ebe7e5b6ed86`.

## 1. Outcome and scope

Expand the existing subject-based notes and LaTeX compendium using the supplied
CFA topic outlines to identify gaps. Organize the resulting knowledge by
financial concepts, learning objectives and prerequisites. Identifiers, paths,
navigation and coverage tracking must be independent of any curriculum provider,
examination level, edition year or reading sequence. Substantive sources are
expected to include non-CFA books, papers, standards and other suitable material.
The user has the topic outlines, not the full curriculum PDFs. Every integration
slice therefore includes searching for, obtaining and reviewing suitable sources
before writing or extending its substantive content. Full curriculum access is
not an implementation prerequisite.

Use **the supplied 2027 outlines as the initial scope reference**, including the
Level III common core and all three pathways: Portfolio Management, Private
Markets and Private Wealth. Preserve 2026 Level III for source comparison. These
years and levels describe external outline versions; they do not version or
partition the canonical notes. Later outlines or different textbooks can map
to the same objectives without renaming them.

The observable result is:

- All **203 modules and 1,030 learning outcome statement (LOS) bullets** in the
  deduplicated source inventory are traceable through a crosswalk to neutral
  learning objectives. These are source-item counts, not prescribed counts of
  internal topics, objectives, entries or files.
- Existing material is reused or extended; missing explanations, worked examples
  and case analyses are written in the relevant subject chapters.
- Foundations lead into analysis, valuation, portfolio and client decisions
  through explicit prerequisite links, independent of examination levels.
- Subject navigation, citations, entry evidence, pathway notes and the compiled
  PDF agree. Coverage and study progress remain separate.

This plan covers the supplied topic outlines. It does not establish coverage of
practical skills modules, examination questions or other material absent from
those outlines. They seed the initial coverage requirements; they do not dictate
the source used to explain a concept. Sourcing substantive readings is part of
implementation. No curriculum PDFs, research results, guide files or existing
entries were changed during this audit.

