### 2.3 Existing repository coverage

The current compendium has **254 entries across 20 chapter wrappers**, out of
38 available guide sections, plus **63 pathway notes and 89 reading notes**.
The checker reports zero structural problems, and all nine existing unit tests
pass. Neither result measures CFA coverage or establishes that the user has
mastered a subject.

The classifications below concern the current compiled notes, not the much
larger reading catalogue. “Partial” identifies useful material to inspect and
reuse; it is not an LOS-level completion claim. “Absent” means no dedicated
treatment was found through the entry inventory, subject searches and inspection
of nearby entries. A full 1,030-row semantic crosswalk remains implementation work.

| Area | Finding and concrete existing evidence | Integration work |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Quantitative methods | **Partial.** `def:interest-rate-measures` in `compendium/rates-credit-mortgages/interest-rates.tex`; covariance estimation and GARCH in `compendium/portfolio-construction-risk/risk-models.tex`. `common-toolkit/section.tex` is absent. | Add probability, distributions, estimation, hypothesis testing, regression and simulation foundations. Reuse finance-specific return/PV and risk entries. Extend econometrics and ML for Level II. |
| Economics | **Partial.** `def:macroeconomic-indicators` in `compendium/multi-asset-systematic-macro/macro-regimes-data.tex`, policy in `rates-credit-mortgages/monetary-policy-curves.tex`, FX in `currency-markets/macro-fx.tex`. | Add firms and market structures, fiscal policy, trade, growth and other outline gaps; connect these foundations to the existing macro/FX applications. |
| Financial statement analysis | **Absent as a subject.** `def:financial-data-types` describes accounting information as lagged data, not statement analysis. The guide's `equity-events-fundamentals` pathway has no corresponding pathway note. | Add the three statements, accounting treatments, ratios, reporting quality and forecasting; then intercorporate investments, compensation, multinational operations and financial institutions. |
| Corporate issuers | **Absent as issuer finance.** `compendium/corporate-credit-capital/credit-default-swaps.tex` contains a CDS mechanism, not capital budgeting or issuer governance. | Add ownership/stakeholders, governance, working capital, investment decisions, capital structure, distributions, cost of capital and restructuring. |
| Equity investments | **Partial.** `meth:equity-index-valuation` is a top-down index screen. `systematic-equities/signal-combination.tex` contains only two signal/forecast entries. | Add company and industry analysis, analyst reporting, DCF/DDM, free cash flow, multiples, residual income and private-company valuation. Link fundamentals to systematic signals without equating the two. |
| Fixed income | **Partial, with reusable rates material.** `rates-credit-mortgages/interest-rates.tex` covers quotations, forwards, swaps, duration and convexity. Sovereign and CDS entries are selective. `securitised-structured-credit/section.tex` is absent. | Fill bond instrument, credit and securitisation foundations; add arbitrage-free valuation, embedded options and credit models. Reconcile CFA teaching conventions with existing multi-curve assumptions. |
| Derivatives | **Partial, with substantial reuse.** `def:derivative-contracts`, `res:cost-of-carry-forward-price` and `volatility-options/option-pricing.tex`, plus rates and commodity hedging entries. | Map payoff, replication, valuation and hedge objectives separately; add missing binomial, contingent-claim and strategy examples. Existing advanced models do not establish introductory coverage. |
| Alternative investments | **Partial overall.** Commodity and digital-asset chapters exist. `private-capital-allocator/section.tex` and `real-asset-investor/section.tex` are absent. | Add fund structures, fees, private capital, property, infrastructure and hedge-fund analysis. Use existing guide homes for private-market and real-asset content. |
| Portfolio management and Level III Portfolio Management | **Partial, with substantial reuse.** Portfolio construction, risk and performance files contain mean–variance, Black–Litterman, risk measures, `def:return-measures` and `meth:gips-composite-presentation`. Execution chapters are well populated. | Add investor objectives/constraints, behavioral finance, institutional mandates, allocation decisions, manager selection and outcome-specific attribution/strategy gaps. |
| Professional ethics | **Absent as CFA ethics.** `def:inside-information` in `execution-market-making/market-making-models.tex` is a narrowly scoped legal treatment. | Add the Code, each Standard I–VII, level-specific applications and the Asset Manager Code. Legal market-conduct notes and CFA professional standards need explicit distinctions. |
| Level III Private Markets | **Absent as a coherent pathway.** General return measures and corporate credit are supporting material only. | Build private-fund economics, GP/LP incentives, underwriting, private equity/debt, special situations, property and infrastructure using the existing guide's unpopulated sections. |
| Level III Private Wealth | **Absent as a coherent pathway.** General portfolio and return entries do not cover client/family planning. | Add industry/adviser context, human capital, goals, taxes, retirement, wealth protection and transfer, with dated jurisdiction-specific assumptions where relevant. |

### 2.4 Integration constraints discovered

These describe the initial pre-integration baseline. Sections 7 and 8 record
implemented changes and the follow-up audit; Section 3 defines the revised target.

- [Compendium conventions](compendium/README.md) require typed entries, stable
  labels, a Statement and substantive citations with locators. Source-review
  diaries belong in `notes/`, not printed entries.
- [The master document](compendium/compendium.tex) and `compendium/house.tex`
  include only section wrappers named in `\qfsections`. Missing wrappers are
  silently skipped. An unreferenced topic file can exist without being printed.
- [The checker](tools/check_compendium.py) accepts chapter keys only from the
  pinned guide, and pathway note filenames only from guide sections/pathways.
  Every entry must be listed in at least one pathway note.
- The checker accepts extra bibliographic keys from
  `compendium/front/sources.tex`. There is no need to modify the guide catalogue
  to cite curriculum readings.
- The checker does not currently validate CFA outcomes, source-file hashes,
  the entry-evidence schema, general Markdown links or LaTeX input reachability.
- `guide/` is a separate pinned Git submodule. Editing its generated documents or
  inventing guide pathway IDs in local notes would create an inconsistent state.
  (Superseded on 2026-10-18, Section 15: `guide/` is now a committed copy of the
  same pinned commit, not a submodule; the rule against editing it still applies.)
- [The coverage log](notes/log.md) and existing pathway notes distinguish draft
  entries, consolidation and mastery. Preserve that distinction.

## 3. Proposed integration design

### 3.1 Subject chapters remain canonical

Naming rule: no maintained folder, filename, module, function, class, variable,
route, objective ID, entry label or citation key may contain `cfa` in any casing.
Use financial subjects and operations instead. Examples: `cash-flow-analysis.tex`,
`check_coverage.py`, `load_objectives()`, `validate_coverage()` and
`reconcile_outline_items()`. This rule applies to helper scripts and tests too.
CFA may appear in accurate source titles, provenance values and explanatory
content about that institution's standards; it is not an implementation namespace.

Keep the current compendium chapter order and concept labels. Extend existing topic
files when the concept already has an appropriate home. Add a separate entry
only for a distinct idea or application. A shared entry can satisfy several
objectives, but each objective needs its own coverage rationale and required
depth. Use subject names for files and preserve existing concept labels.

Populate the registered compendium sections for `common-toolkit`,
`securitised-structured-credit`, `private-capital-allocator` and
`real-asset-investor` as their content is written. They are already named in the
master and accepted by the checker. Use relevant existing specialist pathways,
such as `equity-events-fundamentals`, `private-equity-vc`,
`pe-performance-measurement` and `property-valuation`, after checking their
actual guide outcomes.

Treat the following four subjects as ordinary compendium chapters with exactly
the same status as every other chapter:

| Section key | Purpose | Current placement in the master |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `financial-statement-analysis` | Accounting, reporting quality and statement analysis | Before `systematic-equities` |
| `corporate-finance` | Issuer decisions, financing and governance | After financial statements, before systematic equities |
| `professional-ethics` | Professional responsibilities, conflicts, conduct and applicable standards | After `research-engineering-validation` |
| `private-wealth-management` | Individual and family wealth decisions | After `portfolio-risk-financing` |

Use the existing ordered `\qfsections` list in `compendium/compendium.tex` as the
single authority for all compendium section IDs and chapter order. It currently
contains 42 unique keys, including these four. Do not introduce a separate
supplement category or a second chapter registry. The pinned guide remains a
reading/pathway reference; it no longer decides which compendium chapters are
permitted.

Replace the union of guide sections and `notes/local-sections.json` with a
shared loader for the master section list, used by both checkers. Accepted
subject-overview note keys come from that list; existing specialist pathway
keys can still come from the guide. Reject duplicate/malformed section IDs,
undeclared content folders and ambiguous collisions with specialist pathway
IDs. A missing or malformed master declaration is a validation failure, not an
invitation to fall back to the old two-registry model.

Remove `notes/local-sections.json`, its special loader/constant/test category,
and active documentation describing some chapters as local or supplemental.
Preserve current IDs, content, source evidence and the historical execution
record. Do not modify the pinned guide submodule to make these subjects valid.

Each populated section needs a `section.tex` wrapper, its topic inputs and an
overview note. Verify that every typed entry is reachable through its wrapper
and the master, rather than merely present on disk. Registered sections with no
content can remain planned until their first substantive entry; do not create
empty printed chapters solely to satisfy the registry. The current 21 populated
chapters and the remaining planned subjects use this same rule.

### 3.2 Files and responsibilities

All new paths below are **planned**, not existing deliverables of this audit.

| Path | Responsibility |
| --- | --- |
| `notes/coverage/README.md` | Subject-based entry point, coverage legend, source limitations and links to the learning roadmap and source crosswalk |
| `notes/coverage/objectives.json` | Canonical subject/topic/objective identities, prerequisites, required depth, entry mappings, coverage status and verification references |
| `notes/coverage/outline-manifest.json` | External outline identities, provider/version metadata, local paths, page counts, full SHA-256 and verified external URLs when available; initially the eight supplied PDFs |
| `notes/coverage/outline-crosswalk.json` | External module/bullet inventory and many-to-many mappings to neutral objective IDs; provider, year and level appear only as source metadata |
| `notes/coverage/learning-roadmap.md` | Subject and prerequisite navigation through foundations, analysis, valuation, portfolio decisions, private markets and wealth planning |
| `notes/coverage/source-changes.md` | Source-version comparisons and their impact on objectives, including the initial 2026-to-2027 outline comparison |
| `reading_materials/outlines/outline-<sha256-prefix>.pdf` | Neutral local filenames for the supplied outline copies; preserve original paths/names as manifest provenance and retain the existing Git ignore policy |
| `compendium/compendium.tex` | Single ordered section declaration for all compendium chapters, consumed by the build and both validators |
| `notes/pathways/<section-or-specialist-key>.md` | Subject overview, existing/proposed entry labels and actual study status |
| `notes/readings/<source-key>.md` | Findings and locators from substantive readings, following the existing format |
| `compendium/<subject>/<topic>.tex` and `section.tex` | Canonical explanations and their inclusion in the PDF |
| `compendium/front/sources.tex` | Extra bibliographic registrations for the sources actually used |
| `notes/entry-evidence.json`, `source-review-records.md`, `source-review-index.md` | Reuse the existing provenance system; do not create a competing review ledger |
| `tools/check_coverage.py` | Small structural validator and subject/objective coverage report; separate source-crosswalk reconciliation and optional outline-file verification |
| `tools/test_check_coverage.py` | Focused tests for the neutral coverage and crosswalk contracts |

Every new printed entry must still appear in an accepted subject pathway or
section-overview note. Keep the substantive teaching in LaTeX; the roadmap
links, sequences and identifies gaps. Source-specific navigation can be derived
from the crosswalk when needed, without becoming a canonical directory or route.

`objectives.json` owns internal objective status. The crosswalk owns source-item
associations and any unmapped requirements. Report unique objective coverage
separately from source-item coverage. Avoid manually duplicating statuses in
Markdown or treating multiple outline references as multiple completed concepts.

### 3.3 Inventory and coverage contract

Keep internal knowledge identities separate from external source occurrences:

| Record | Required information |
| --- | --- |
| Topic | Stable subject-based ID; title; owning section; scope; prerequisite topic/objective IDs |
| Learning objective | Stable semantic ID; topic ID; capability to demonstrate; required operations and depth; entry labels; coverage status; missing requirements; next action, including source discovery where needed; relevant reading-note/review/example references |
| External outline item | Manifest ID; exact module heading and source order; bullet ordinal; concise paraphrase and original command verbs; PDF/printed page(s); source-specific level/pathway metadata where applicable |
| Crosswalk mapping | External item reference; one or more objective IDs; explanation of how the objectives meet its requirements; any residual gap; duplicate source occurrences where applicable |
| Substantive evidence | Source citation key and locator, plus entry-evidence/review record and worked example or case when needed |

Examples of neutral identities:

| Kind | Example |
| --- | --- |
| Topic | `topic:return-measurement` |
| Learning objective | `objective:compare-time-and-money-weighted-returns` |
| Another objective | `objective:value-equity-with-free-cash-flow` |
| Existing printed entry | `def:return-measures` |
| Planned topic file | `compendium/financial-statement-analysis/cash-flow-analysis.tex` |

No topic or objective ID contains a provider name, examination level, year,
reading number or source bullet ordinal. Use neutral source IDs such as
`outline:7099c7688942`; a source item's locator can be a manifest reference plus
module/bullet location. Provider, edition and level are descriptive metadata,
not ID components. A source item's identity is never the objective's identity.

Persist semantic IDs when changing readings, adding a source or refreshing an
outline edition. New editions create source records and crosswalk changes.
They create new objectives only when the required capability changes. Merge
equivalent capabilities across sources; split genuinely different capabilities.
If a previously implemented objective must split or merge, retain a supersession
pointer and update its mappings rather than reusing its ID for a new meaning.

Objectives can exist without any outline association and can be supported
entirely by non-CFA sources. Neither provider nor examination level is a required
field in an internal topic/objective. Genuine names of standards, such as the
CFA Code or GIPS, remain accurate in the content and bibliographic references;
neutral identifiers do not make provider-specific rules universal.

Represent both dimensions independently:

- **Coverage:** `missing`, `partial`, `covered`.
- **Next action:** `reuse`, `extend`, `write`, `obtain-source`, or `review`.

`Covered` requires inspection of the mapped text against **all operations in
the internal objective**. A calculation objective needs a worked calculation and
interpretation; a comparison needs the meaningful distinctions; a
recommend/justify objective needs a decision under stated client constraints.
Merely linking a title, adding a citation, passing a checker or writing a
definition is insufficient.

Record which part remains missing in every partial mapping. Do not mark an
outcome covered while its substantive source or necessary example is still
unresolved. Several entries may jointly cover one objective; one entry may
support several objectives. Separately review each external bullet against its
mapped objectives: it is covered only when their verified capabilities satisfy
every operation in that bullet and no residual gap remains. Linking a broad
objective to an outline bullet does not automatically establish source coverage.

Keep learner attempts and mastery separate from editorial coverage, using the
existing study-log conventions. Do not assign mastery dates on the user's behalf.
For the initial audit map, existing entries are candidates for reuse until
reviewed against the specific capability; this plan assigns no completed
objective or source-item count. The number of internal objectives will emerge
from semantic grouping, and is not fixed at 1,030.

### 3.4 Sources and content depth

Record all eight local outline hashes before extraction. Reconcile module
headings and bullets against the PDFs, especially page breaks, split titles,
checkboxes and the Level III boundary. PDF extraction currently inserts spaces
inside words such as “Performance”; normalization must not alter meaning.

Use the outline to explain why a capability was included. Select substantive
sources for their authority, clarity and relevance to the financial concept,
regardless of curriculum provider. Prefer suitable existing books, papers and
primary standards; no CFA textbook is required merely because an objective was
discovered in a CFA outline. The supplied folder contains outlines, not full
readings. Inventory available sources first and record what further material is
needed. If a proposed reading is unavailable, use an equally suitable accessible
source; otherwise leave the affected objective partial while others proceed.
Sources may be readable HTML, papers, books, standards or other appropriate
documents; a PDF is not required. Searching and reading are part of each topic's
implementation work, not a one-time bibliography exercise before integration.

Register edition-specific source keys in `compendium/front/sources.tex`, reuse
existing catalogue keys where they refer to the same source, and cite precise
locators. An outline page must not stand in for a derivation or a provision in
a standard. Write original explanations and examples; retain local source PDFs
under the existing ignore rules. A source URL, when recorded, must be verified,
not inferred from its local filename.

Apply the naming rule to new bibliographic keys as well: use a work/subject key
such as `professional-conduct-standards-<edition>` or the neutral outline ID.
Keep the institution's actual name in the bibliographic reference, not the key.
Do not relabel institution-specific standards as generic rules.

Organize depth by capability, independently of examination levels:

- **Foundations:** definition, assumptions, units, mechanics and a clear calculation
  or interpretation where required.
- **Analysis and valuation:** application, competing methods, model diagnostics,
  linked statements and sensitivity to assumptions.
- **Portfolio and client decisions:** context, constraints, alternatives, a justified
  recommendation and its monitoring or implementation implications.

These are navigation descriptions, not mandatory ID prefixes or one-to-one
aliases for external levels. A topic may span several depths and source outlines.

Preserve material conventions: timing of cash flows, total versus excess returns,
nominal versus real rates, currency quotation, compounding, clean/dirty prices,
duration units, accounting framework and reporting period. Distinguish simplified
teaching models from the assumptions in the existing research/execution notes.
For tax, legal, professional-standard or accounting applications, name the
relevant edition, jurisdiction and effective period after checking the source.
Synthetic teaching examples establish mechanics, not empirical strategy returns.

### 3.5 Source discovery and review in every integration slice

Apply this loop to each coherent topic/objective slice in Batches 0–6. A slice
can share sources across several objectives; it need not run one search per
bullet or repeat unchanged searches for every small edit.

1. **Define the evidence needed.** List the slice's neutral objective IDs,
   required explanations/calculations/cases and gaps in existing entries. Check
   the pinned catalogue, local reading material and prior source-review records
   for useful starting points. A catalogue listing alone does not prove access
   to the relevant text or support for a claim.
2. **Search for suitable material.** Search the web using subject terminology,
   required methods and assumptions rather than examination labels. Prefer
   original research, author/university-hosted material, authoritative textbooks
   available to read, and official standards, regulator, central-bank, statistical
   agency or exchange documentation as appropriate. Use primary sources for
   technical claims and current rules. Reuse good existing sources, but search
   for missing coverage, unclear assumptions and relevant updates in this slice.
3. **Open and read the relevant content.** Confirm authorship, title, version,
   publication/effective date, access and the precise sections supporting the
   proposed claims. Search snippets, citation lists and abstracts do not establish
   that a derivation, rule or full argument was reviewed. For inaccessible
   material, look for an authorized public version or a suitable alternative.
4. **Assess sufficiency before drafting.** Check that the chosen material covers
   the objective's depth and matches its units, timing, jurisdiction and other
   assumptions. Resolve material disagreements explicitly. Obtain another source
   when it answers a specific uncertainty; do not impose a fixed source quota.
   If a necessary fact remains unsupported, retain `missing` or `partial` and
   `obtain-source` with the exact gap, while continuing independent objectives.
5. **Write, cite and verify.** Draft original explanations and examples from the
   reviewed material. Register neutral citation keys, cite exact page/section
   locators, perform the relevant numerical or case checks, and connect the
   objective to its entries and review evidence. Outline coverage alone cannot
   replace substantive evidence.
6. **Record a compact source handoff.** In existing reading notes and review
   records, retain the search date and useful query, selected source identity,
   URL/DOI or local reference, edition/date, sections actually read, objectives
   supported and unresolved access/coverage limitations. Record rejected sources
   only when the reason matters for future work. Link these records from the
   objectives and entry evidence instead of creating a separate search service
   or copying full browsing histories into the notes.

Each content slice is done only after both its source review and its integration
checks pass. Its completion report identifies sources actually read, entries
changed, objectives covered and remaining gaps. A downloaded document or a
promising search result is not completed source review. Prefer accessible
alternatives over making progress depend on unavailable curriculum books.

For unchanged material with sufficient prior evidence, record why that evidence
still applies. At final reconciliation, search again for unresolved gaps,
changed claims and time-sensitive sources; do not repeat every earlier search
or rerun unrelated research by habit.

## 4. Implementation sequence

The checkpoints below define independently reviewable batches. They are ordered
by prerequisite and integration risk. The source counts below preserve the
agreed coverage scope, while the work is organized by subject and capability.
Do not create one LaTeX file or one computational test per source bullet.

The source-discovery loop in Section 3.5 is part of every batch's work and
acceptance, with the following focus. These are source categories to search,
not claims that particular documents have already been found or reviewed.

| Batch | Required source work before or alongside integration |
| --- | --- |
| 0 — Inventory | Inspect existing bibliographic coverage, search for sources for the first pilot objectives and identify likely source gaps by subject. Record candidates separately from reviewed evidence; do not attempt to assemble every future reading before proceeding. |
| 1 — Pilot | Search, obtain and read sufficient material for the return-measurement and financial-statement examples; prove both source-to-entry journeys using the sources actually accessed. |
| 2 — Foundations | Search per subject slice for mathematical/statistical treatments, economics sources, accounting guidance and reports, instrument documentation and professional standards. Read the sections needed before writing that slice. |
| 3 — Analysis and valuation | Search for derivations, valuation methods, model assumptions and worked applications in original papers, authoritative texts and primary accounting/market documents; resolve differences from existing entries. |
| 4 — Portfolio decisions | Search for allocation, institutional mandate, attribution, manager-selection and execution evidence; verify applicable performance and conduct standards and the assumptions behind cases. |
| 5 — Private markets and wealth | Search separately for fund economics and underwriting sources, and for client planning, tax, inheritance and professional guidance. Verify jurisdiction and effective dates where the conclusions depend on them. |
| 6 — Reconciliation | Search for outstanding evidence gaps and refresh sources affected by changed claims or time-sensitive rules. Reuse sufficient unchanged evidence with a recorded rationale. |

### Batch 0 — Freeze the source inventory and build the crosswalk

1. Create the neutral manifest and coverage entry point. Record the eight full
   hashes and original paths below as provenance. Relocate the supplied copies
   to `reading_materials/outlines/outline-<sha256-prefix>.pdf`, initially using
   12 digest characters and extending them if necessary to avoid collisions.
   Verify full hashes before/after, update local references, and remove the old
   directory only once it is empty. Do not move unrelated files or alter the
   ignore policy. This relocation is planned work, not part of this plan edit.
2. Extract the five scope-reference outlines, reconcile their counts and
   consolidate the shared Level III core. Keep all three source occurrences.
3. Inventory all 203 source modules / 1,030 bullets in the crosswalk. Group their
   required capabilities into subject-based topics and neutral objectives,
   retaining each source locator and residual gap. Do not copy source headings
   or levels into internal IDs or assume one objective per bullet.
4. Map candidate existing entries and substantive sources to objectives, inspect
   them before assigning covered status, and record explicit next actions.
5. Create the subject/prerequisite roadmap and source-version comparison.
   Search for pilot readings, record access/coverage gaps and distinguish source
   candidates from material actually read. Use suitable non-CFA sources.

Acceptance: totals match Sections 2.1–2.2; each module and bullet is traceable;
core is counted once; distinct standards and levels remain distinct in the
source crosswalk. Neutral topics/objectives can span those source items. All
maintained paths and identifiers follow the naming rule, with original names
retained only as provenance. This finishes the **inventory**, not content or
learner mastery. Pilot sources are identified with actual access status; full
source discovery for later subjects remains part of their respective batches.

### Batch 1 — Integrate chapter ownership and prove two reader journeys

Depends on Batch 0.

1. Use the master document as the single section authority, following Section
   3.1. Update both checkers and conventions; keep the guide submodule pinned.
   The earlier two-registry implementation is superseded by the Section 8 correction.
2. Implement the coverage validator using the standard library and existing
   parsing helpers where useful. Validate data shape, unique IDs, source
   references, objective and crosswalk mappings, entry labels, section ownership
   and status requirements. Report missing/partial/covered by subject/objective;
   report source-item reconciliation separately.
3. Complete the source-discovery/review pass for both pilot objectives, then reuse
   `def:return-measures` for
   `objective:compare-time-and-money-weighted-returns`, adding only any missing
   calculation/interpretation after review. Map relevant source bullets to this
   objective without automatically awarding coverage to every related item.
4. Write one sourced financial-statement analysis concept plus a small original
   worked example, register its new chapter, and connect its reading note,
   pathway note, entry evidence and neutral objective.
5. Follow each journey from the subject roadmap to the entry in the compiled PDF and
   onward to the substantive source locator. Check both an existing chapter and
   the financial-statement chapter actually appear in the PDF.

Acceptance: the two journeys work with appropriately cited substantive sources,
existing checks and focused new tests pass, and the added chapter is included in
the build. Reading/review records identify the sources actually accessed and
sections read. Verify that an objective can be supported by a non-CFA source, have
multiple outline associations or no association, and retain its ID when a source
changes. This validates the integration method before a large writing effort.

### Batch 2 — Foundations, financial statements and professional ethics

Depends on Batch 1. Start ethics early and develop it alongside the analytical
material. Use coherent topic batches with the following concrete deliverables:

| Work package | Planned homes and content | Decisive content check |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Quantitative foundations | `common-toolkit/`: distributions, statistics, sampling, inference, regression, simulation and data-science overview; link existing PV and return entries | A small return/PV calculation, an independently checked inference example, and an interpreted regression result cover the operations actually requested |
| Economics | Extend `multi-asset-systematic-macro/`, `currency-markets/` and rates policy notes with firms, cycles, fiscal/monetary policy, geopolitics, trade and capital flows | Explain a firm/market-structure case and trace a policy/FX scenario using explicit assumptions |
| Statements and issuer decisions | New `financial-statement-analysis/` and `corporate-finance/` topics covering accounting foundations and issuer decisions | Reconcile a simple set of financial statements; interpret ratios and cash flows; explain an investment/financing decision with consistent timing |
| Equity analysis | Extend `systematic-equities/` with instrument/ownership foundations, company and industry analysis, DCF/growth/multiples, forecasting and research reports | Trace earnings/cash-flow assumptions into an equity value and identify sensitivity and limitations |
| Fixed income and derivatives | Extend rates, government/corporate credit and options; activate `securitised-structured-credit/` | Reconcile price/yield and risk units; show a securitisation cash-flow mechanism and replication/payoff examples required by the outlines |
| Alternatives and portfolio foundations | Activate private-capital/real-asset chapters; extend commodity, digital-asset and portfolio chapters for structures, fees, risk/return, investor planning and behavioral biases | Work through fees/returns and an investor objectives-and-constraints case; distinguish valuation from investability |
| Professional ethics | New `professional-ethics/`: trust, professional responsibilities, conflicts and application of the specific Code/Standards identified in the source outline | Original situations identify the governing standard, explain the conduct and describe an appropriate response using sourced rules |

Acceptance: the foundation objectives are covered at their required depth.
Reconcile the initial source scope against all 102 modules / 340 bullets of the
Level I outline, reporting residual gaps. These remain external source counts;
they do not determine the number or names of the internal objectives.

### Batch 3 — Financial analysis, valuation and model diagnostics

Depends on the relevant foundations; unrelated topic batches can proceed
independently once their own prerequisites and sources are available.

- **Quantitative methods and economics:** multiple regression, model fit,
  misspecification, extensions, time series, ML and big-data workflows; FX
  equilibrium and growth. Use `common-toolkit/`, `ml-alternative-data/`, macro,
  currency and existing validation chapters. An advanced backtest entry does
  not substitute for a missing regression diagnostic or forecast calculation.
- **Financial statements and corporate issuers:** intercorporate holdings,
  post-employment/share-based compensation, multinational accounting, financial
  institutions, quality/integrated analysis, distributions, ESG, advanced cost
  of capital and restructuring. Preserve the relevant accounting frameworks.
- **Equity:** valuation process, DDM, FCFF/FCFE, price and enterprise multiples,
  residual income and private-company valuation. Connect statement adjustments
  to model inputs and independently reconcile cash-flow/value bridges.
- **Fixed income and derivatives:** term structure/dynamics, arbitrage-free
  valuation, embedded options, credit models/CDS, forward commitments and
  contingent claims. Verify a tree or other applicable numerical valuation
  against a small independent calculation and stated assumptions.
- **Alternatives:** commodities, real-estate structures, listed property and
  hedge-fund strategies; compare economic exposures, fees, liquidity and return
  measurement with the existing market-mechanics material.
- **Portfolio management:** economics/investment markets, active management,
  ETFs, multifactor models, market risk, backtesting and simulation. Reuse the
  existing portfolio, ETF and validation entries where they satisfy the objectives.
- **Ethics:** deepen professional judgment in analytical and client situations;
  retain separate source mappings for all nine modules in the analysis outline.

Acceptance: analysis and valuation objectives are covered, including calculation,
interpretation and limitations. Reconcile all 51 modules / 382 bullets of the
Level II source outline through the crosswalk. Reuse links alone do not establish
either objective or source-item coverage.

### Batch 4 — Asset allocation, portfolio management and institutional mandates

Depends on relevant valuation, risk and portfolio foundations.

Build shared portfolio capabilities once, linking to:

- `multi-asset-systematic-macro/` and `portfolio-construction-risk/` for capital
  market expectations, governance, economic balance sheets, allocation methods,
  real-world constraints and rebalancing;
- equity, rates, alternatives and portfolio chapters for mandate design,
  institutional objectives, liquidity, trading costs and the institutional SWF
  case; `private-wealth-management/` for the core private-client overview;
- `portfolio-construction-risk/performance-evaluation.tex` and related topics
  for attribution/appraisal, manager selection and GIPS;
- options, rates and currency chapters for derivative strategies and currency
  management;
- `professional-ethics/` for portfolio-management conduct and the Asset Manager Code.

Then cover all eight Portfolio Management pathway modules: index-based equity;
active equity strategies; active equity construction; liability-driven and
index-based fixed income; yield-curve strategies; active credit; trade strategy
and execution; and the institutional endowment case.

Use original decision cases to demonstrate the required recommendations. State
objectives, constraints, alternatives, trade-offs and the chosen implementation.
Reuse existing execution/TCA material while checking that it answers the
mandate-level question. Do not reproduce supplied examination cases as new work.

Acceptance: portfolio decision objectives meet the required depth, and the
source crosswalk reconciles the core's 183 bullets and Portfolio Management's
64 bullets. Shared objectives are counted once internally; their source
associations remain distinct. Cases or optimization equations alone do not
establish completion.

### Batch 5 — Private markets and private wealth

Depends on relevant foundations and portfolio concepts; these two subjects can be
implemented independently. Both remain in scope even though Portfolio
Management is the closest match to the current repository.

**Private markets** (source scope: 7 modules / 34 bullets). Use `private-capital-allocator/` for
structures, GP/investor perspectives, private equity, debt and special
situations, and `real-asset-investor/` for property and infrastructure. Reuse
corporate finance/credit entries for supporting mechanisms. Include fund
cash-flow/fee calculations, DPI/RVPI/TVPI, investment-process and exit decisions,
venture/growth/buyout valuation, private-debt analysis and asset-specific due
diligence. Use one small dated cash-flow example to reconcile investor returns
and fees, and distinct underwriting examples where the economics differ.
Do not treat a commodity futures note as real-asset underwriting.

**Private wealth** (source scope: 7 modules / 27 bullets). Use `private-wealth-management/` for the
industry, working with wealthy clients, wealth planning, investment planning,
preservation, advice and transfer. Link existing portfolio/risk material and the
new corporate/ethics chapters. Use original client/family cases involving human
and financial capital, goals, liquidity, taxes, retirement, inflation/FX,
business ownership and transfers. Identify the jurisdiction and date of any
specific legal/tax assumptions; generic examples must say which assumptions
are hypothetical. A portfolio optimizer alone cannot satisfy advisory or
intergenerational planning objectives.

Acceptance: the private-market and private-wealth objectives are reviewed at
their required decision depth, including non-trading subjects. Reconcile all
34 and 27 source bullets without relabeling gaps as optional.

### Batch 6 — Reconcile and publish the completed reference locally

Depends on completed content batches.

1. Resolve duplicate explanations and inconsistent notation; preserve stable
   labels and historical source-review records when moving material.
2. Reconcile objective statuses, source mappings and evidence against the final text. Check
   every printed entry added or changed is reachable from a chapter wrapper/master
   and is listed in an accepted subject note.
3. Refresh navigation, source bibliography and the existing evidence records.
   Keep study-log mastery dates tied to actual learner work.
4. Run the checks and final build below. Inspect changed chapter openings and
   pages with dense tables, equations, cases and cross-references.
5. Retain the compiled compendium under the existing repository convention;
   report remaining gaps by neutral objective ID and, where applicable, source locator.

Do not mark full integration complete unless the content acceptance criteria
below are met. A usable intermediate release can be delivered with explicit
partial coverage, but it is not the completed integration of the agreed scope.

## 5. Verification and completion criteria

### Current commands

Run from the repository root after integration/code changes:

```sh
make sync
make source-check
```

Build after LaTeX content or inclusion changes:

```sh
make pdf
```

Check coverage and verify the local outline copies:

```sh
make check-coverage
make verify-outlines
```

`make check` combines source validation, outline hashes and the PDF build. The
Make targets and locked uv environment were added in Section 12; commands in
earlier progress records describe their historical environments. The default coverage check validates committed
metadata and links without requiring ignored local PDFs. Explicit
`--verify-outlines` requires the source copies and checks their hashes; a missing
or mismatched file must be reported as a source-verification failure, not
silently accepted. Neither mode awards semantic coverage or learner mastery.

Keep the implementation small. Add tests that catch actual integration failures:

- All section owners come from the master declaration; duplicate/malformed keys,
  undeclared content, unreachable typed entries and a missing master declaration
  fail. Planned sections without content do not require empty printed chapters.
- A mapped label that does not exist, wrong source reference, duplicate objective
  ID, invalid status or unsupported owner fails.
- A source change preserves objective IDs; multiple outline items can map to one
  objective; objectives without outline links and with non-CFA evidence validate.
  Canonical records do not require provider, year or examination-level fields.
- A maintained folder, file, function, class, variable, ID or citation key using
  the prohibited provider token is rejected during the naming review. Original
  filenames recorded as provenance values are not maintained paths.
- Repeated Level III core occurrences do not inflate active totals, while
  identical wording in distinct standards/levels remains separate.
- An unreviewed outline mapping never reports covered merely because its
  objectives are covered. Preserve explicit mapping-review evidence and gaps.
- Mapped content must be typed entry labels, not arbitrary chapter/figure labels.
  A covered objective's mapped entries, entry-evidence references and substantive
  source records must be structurally consistent and nonempty. Semantic
  sufficiency still requires review.
- Source occurrence counts reconcile independently to each active outline's
  manifest counts, counting shared core occurrences in each source file but
  only once in the canonical crosswalk. Dropping an item and reducing the
  crosswalk's own counters must fail against the unchanged manifest.
- Explicit source verification detects missing or changed PDF bytes.

Do not add tests that merely search for prose. Use hand-reconciled numerical
examples for new calculations and carefully reasoned cases for qualitative
outcomes. A change to only navigation does not require rerunning historical
research; renew numerical evidence only when its inputs, formulas or assumptions
change. When a check fails, identify whether the cause is extraction, metadata,
content, source access or the build before broadening the work.

Full content acceptance:

- [x] Source crosswalk reconciles to 203 modules / 1,030 bullets, with the common
      core counted once and all three pathways retained as scope evidence.
      (Batch 0, 2026-10-18: inventory and mapping only; item coverage is separate.)
- [ ] Every in-scope neutral objective is covered at its required depth with
      entries and substantive source locators; every source bullet's requirements
      are satisfied through the crosswalk with no residual gaps.
- [ ] Each integration slice records source discovery and substantive review,
      or justified reuse of sufficient prior evidence. No objective is marked
      covered from an outline, search snippet or inaccessible citation alone.
- [ ] Canonical paths, filenames, functions, classes, variables, route names,
      entry labels, objective IDs and citation keys follow the neutral naming rule.
      Changing a source does not rename the concept or objective.
- [ ] Shared concepts have a canonical home; advanced reuse does not skip
      introductory explanation or the required depth of application.
- [x] All populated subjects are ordinary compendium chapters under the single
      master declaration, and their topic inputs are reachable through navigation.
      (2026-10-18: 21 populated chapters; enforced by `tools/check_compendium.py`.
      Recheck as each planned section is populated.)
- [ ] Citation, label, pathway, section and coverage checks pass; the PDF builds
      without unresolved references or unaddressed layout defects on changed pages.
- [ ] Original examples and numerical checks are consistent with declared
      conventions; source-review records identify what was actually checked.
- [ ] Reading availability, editorial coverage and learner mastery are reported
      separately. No unsupported “CFA complete” or examination-readiness claim.

