### 9.2 P2 finding: exclude non-income-statement deferred-tax movements

`compendium/financial-statement-analysis/cash-flow-analysis.tex`, audit line 17,
adds total deferred-tax asset/liability balance changes to the cash-tax formula
without restricting them to tax recognised in profit or loss. Those balances
can also change through other comprehensive income (OCI) or directly in equity.

Counterexample: tax expense in profit or loss is 30, comprising current tax 30
and deferred tax zero; tax payable is unchanged; a separate asset revaluation
creates a deferred-tax liability of 10 entirely in OCI. Cash tax paid is 30,
but the printed formula calculates `30 - 0 - 10 = 20`. The existing example
contains no deferred tax, so its passing arithmetic does not detect this error.

Primary evidence: IAS 12 paragraphs 60-63, especially 61A and 62(a), in the
already retrieved official text at
`reading_materials/coverage-pilots/eu_32023R1803.xhtml`; paragraph 61A assigns
tax on OCI/equity items to those locations, and paragraph 62(a) names asset
revaluations. The document is Commission Regulation (EU) 2023/1803,
[official source](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32023R1803).
The local paragraph text was inspected; the IFRS-hosted standard PDF redirected
to a subscription page. No inference from a search snippet was needed.

Correct the formula using an explicit current-tax roll-forward, or limit the
deferred-tax adjustment to the relevant profit-or-loss component and state the
necessary assumptions. Handle or exclude OCI/equity, acquisition, translation
and other non-cash balance movements explicitly. Apply the same stated
simplifying assumptions to the neighboring cash-conversion identities. Add a
small independent counterexample for the OCI-only change, preserving the
existing distributor example and its historical evidence.

Source limitation carried forward: the current FASB Codification has not been
directly checked. The cited 2016 primary amendment and the secondary quotation
are identified in the reading notes. Inaccessibility alone does not establish
that the comparison is wrong; neither does a 2026 website copyright prove its
quoted Codification text is current. Preserve the source vintage and scope in
the entry's Conditions, and avoid claiming current-primary verification that
was not performed. Additional source work should address a specific unresolved
claim rather than repeat an open-ended search for the entire Codification.

