### 10.2 Findings to fix before the next topic entries

All three findings are P2, bounded teaching/model-assumption defects. The
arithmetic for the supplied examples is correct; the checks do not establish
the unrestricted interpretations currently stated in the prose.

1. **Forward lending is not future refinancing at a guaranteed rate.**
   `compendium/common-toolkit/time-value.tex`, audit line 54, and
   `notes/artefacts/time-value-returns/time-value-return-checks.py`, lines
   140-145, treat refinancing one-year borrowing at the implied forward as
   assured. Buying a two-year zero paying 1,000 for 928.1225 and borrowing its
   cost for one year creates cash flows `0, -955.9662, +1000`: a forward loan
   made by the investor, earning 4.6062% between years 1 and 2. Refinancing the
   year-one debt instead requires a new loan or an explicitly contracted
   forward borrowing arrangement. If the then-prevailing rate is 8%, repayment
   is 1,032.4434, not 1,000. Explain the dated forward-lending cash flows, or
   reverse the original trades to illustrate locked forward borrowing. Check
   those cash flows directly; do not substitute the implied rate for an unknown
   future spot rate in a test labelled as a lock-in proof.
2. **State the certainty/definition condition for the Fisher identity.**
   `time-value.tex`, audit lines 4, 8 and 10, calls the relation exact while
   setting inflation to its expectation and describing the real rate as growth
   in purchasing power. The identity is exact for realized inflation, or for
   a specified deterministic inflation scenario/implied-rate definition. It
   does not generally give expected real return under uncertain inflation.
   For fixed nominal return 10% and equally likely inflation of 0% or 20%,
   deflating by mean inflation gives 0%; the mean realized real return is
   `(10% + (1.10/1.20 - 1))/2 = 0.8333%`. Keep the teaching calculation with
   its explicit assumption, and separate it from the stochastic yield model
   and its risk premia. This is a missing condition, not a claim that the
   textbook was misquoted. The already cited
   [Federal Reserve note](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/notes/feds-notes/tips-from-tips-update-and-discussions-20190521.html),
   equations (1)-(4), was independently checked for that model distinction.
3. **Name the yield quotation before halving an annual rate.**
   `time-value.tex`, audit line 21, says to halve the rate for semiannual
   coupons, while the Notation field specifies annual compounding. Halving
   applies to a nominal annual yield quoted with semiannual compounding. An
   effective annual yield instead gives half-year rate `(1+y_eff)^(1/2)-1`.
   At a 10% effective annual yield, cash flows 5 in six months and 105 in one
   year are worth 100.2218584; halving the rate incorrectly gives 100. State
   both conventions explicitly and verify that equivalent quotations produce
   the same price. The source's nominal bond-yield convention is valid; the
   entry must preserve it when generalizing the rule.

These findings were checked through independent scalar calculations and dated
cash-flow reasoning. No entries, coverage data or example scripts were edited
during this audit; only this plan was updated. Preserve existing example values
when they remain valid under the clarified assumptions and renew only the
affected evidence.

