### 11.2 Three bounded P2 teaching corrections

1. **Qualify the unbiased sample-variance claim.**
   `compendium/common-toolkit/descriptive-statistics.tex`, audit lines 6 and
   14, claims that dividing by `N-1` is unbiased without the sampling assumptions
   used in its source. Merely having a stable marginal distribution is not
   sufficient; the Conditions field even discusses dependent monthly returns.
   For a simple counterexample, let `X1=X2=Z`, where `Z` is equally likely to
   be -1 or +1. The marginal variance is 1, but every observed two-point sample
   has sample variance 0. State the i.i.d., finite-variance assumptions (or
   precisely stated sufficient alternatives) for the bias calculation and
   distinguish them from describing a fixed dataset. The cited
   [Pishro-Nik section 8.2.2](https://www.probabilitycourse.com/chapter8/8_2_2_point_estimators_for_mean_and_var.php)
   was checked: its derivation uses the random-sample variance of the mean.
2. **Do not infer central mass from kurtosis.**
   `descriptive-statistics.tex`, audit line 25, says positive excess kurtosis
   means more mass in both the tails and centre than a same-variance normal.
   A fourth standardized moment does not establish that shape. For example,
   assign probability 0.495 to each of -1 and +1 and 0.005 to each of -10 and
   +10. Variance is 1.99 and kurtosis 25.5019, yet there is zero probability
   within half a standard deviation of the mean; the matching normal has
   probability about 0.3829 there. Explain sensitivity to extreme observations
   without asserting a universal central peak or pointwise tail comparison.
   The cited [NIST definition](https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/eda/section3/eda35b.htm)
   supplies the fourth-moment formula and describes a tendency toward outliers;
   it does not support the added central-mass claim.
3. **Distinguish finite sample summaries from population moment existence.**
   `descriptive-statistics.tex`, audit line 25, calls the sample counterpart
   meaningless when a population moment does not exist; `probability.tex`,
   audit line 46, warns against quoting sample variance/kurtosis at all for
   certain Student-t distributions. A finite, nondegenerate sample can still
   have valid descriptive variance and kurtosis. What fails is interpreting
   them as estimates of a finite population moment that does not exist.
   Preserve this distinction consistently with `probability.tex`'s own
   Conditions field. State the general Student-t thresholds for real `nu>0`:
   mean requires `nu>1`, finite variance `nu>2`, skewness `nu>3` and kurtosis
   `nu>4`; separate undefined/infinite moments from finite sample calculations.

Two small notation clarifications belong in the same probability edit, without
changing its correct example values: define the difference between GBM drift
and mean log return (the latter adds half the log variance when computing mean
price), and say that the binomial observations are independent **conditional on
the hypothesis/probability parameter**. In the existing prior mixture,
`P(up,up)=0.25125`, whereas the product of the marginal probabilities is 0.25;
the batch/sequential calculation already uses the correct conditional model.

Keep numerical verification paths, interpreter details and claims about which
script checked a figure in the evidence records. The new introductory sentence
in `descriptive-statistics.tex` prints a check-script path; remove that execution
detail from the compendium in accordance with its existing prose conventions.

Use small direct counterexamples for these corrections and preserve the prior
eight-return and six-cell examples where their numbers remain valid. Do not
expand this into a review of unrelated chapters or a new test infrastructure.

