entries for useful applications; retain their ownership rather than duplicate
them. Integrate the foundational treatment into the existing common-toolkit
chapter, with neutral names and the single master section declaration.

For inference, explain the sampling design and estimand, the central limit
theorem's assumptions, confidence intervals and their repeated-sampling meaning,
null/alternative hypotheses, significance, p-values, Type I/II errors and power.
Choose illustrative parametric and nonparametric tests that answer stated
questions; a nonparametric method still has assumptions. Include chi-square
and F where they support the required variance/test treatment, resolving the
scope note carried forward from the distribution entry. Distinguish exact
finite-sample results from approximations and avoid using i.i.d. uncertainty
formulas for dependent return data without justification.

For simulation, distinguish replaying historical observations, resampling data
and sampling from a specified probability model. Give an original financial
example for each. State the historical-data/reweighting assumptions; bootstrap
sampling unit and dependence limitations; and Monte Carlo distribution, seed,
estimation target and sampling error. A small bounded example of why ordinary
i.i.d. resampling breaks dependence is sufficient; a general backtest engine
or resampling framework is unnecessary.

Use independent hand calculations or exact enumeration where available, and
compare a Monte Carlo estimate with an analytic benchmark using justified
simulation uncertainty. Retain seeds and the actual interpreter/package
versions. Simulation examples establish mechanics, not empirical strategy
performance, and should not tune seeds to manufacture a passing conclusion.

Individually review the six source mappings associated with these objectives
before changing coverage. Update readings, entry evidence, source reviews and
plan progress. Run both checkers, relevant tests and numerical examples using
the documented environment; rebuild and inspect the affected PDF pages.

Stop after these six objectives and the bounded corrections. Do not begin
regression, time-series modelling or the remaining Batch 2 subjects, and do
not recreate the inventory or redesign the checkers.

