### 13.2 Corrections before new content

**P2 — Use one VaR convention and the discrete ES definition.**
`compendium/common-toolkit/simulation-methods.tex`, especially lines 6-12, uses
the second-largest of 20 losses for equal-weight 90% VaR, but the first descending
cumulative weight strictly exceeding 10% for age-weighted VaR. Applying that
weighted rule to uniform weights selects the third-largest loss, 8,400, whereas
the equal-weight example reports 12,800. The existing canonical lower loss
quantile in `compendium/portfolio-construction-risk/risk-models.tex` also gives
8,400. With that convention, both weighting schemes give 8,400 on the current
sample: the claimed one-third fall is caused by changing conventions.

Use the canonical lower loss quantile for the worked comparison. Hull's quoted
rank convention may remain clearly attributed as an alternative convention,
with the difference explained. Do not silently rewrite what the source says.
Retain the data and report no VaR change here, or use a separately declared
example if a weighting effect needs illustration. Do not tune data after the
fact to preserve the original claim.

The same entry defines ES as averaging losses "beyond" VaR, but its stated VaR
of 12,800 would then leave only the 18,600 loss, contradicting its ES of 15,700.
Reference the canonical exact-tail-mass definition, including fractional mass at
the VaR boundary for discrete distributions. ES of 15,700 is correct for the
worst 10% of these 20 equally weighted scenarios. Check uniform weights against
the unweighted quantile at an exact CDF boundary, plus tied losses and a
fractional tail; the current assertions hard-code the inconsistent conventions.
The original source is [Acerbi and Tasche, Definitions 2.1-2.6 and Proposition
3.2](https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/0104295v3), already cited by the canonical
entry. Re-read the relevant Hull passages and this definition when correcting
the entry and evidence.

**P2 — State and satisfy the independent-samples assumption of the F test.**
`compendium/common-toolkit/statistical-inference.tex:27-29` describes normality
but omits independence between the two samples for the ordinary variance-ratio
F test. It then gives a two-sided p-value of 0.008 for the two managers' return
series. Their paired sample correlation is 0.99685; the entry supplies no
independent-sampling model that justifies this reference distribution. A sample
correlation alone is not a proof about population independence, but it makes
the missing assumption especially consequential in this financial example.
The [Penn State summary of the two-variance test](https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat500/Lesson12)
explicitly requires independent samples from normal populations.

Use a separately specified independent normal synthetic-sampling example for
the elementary F test, or explicitly model pairing and source a suitable paired
variance test. Prefer the bounded independent example if pairing is not needed
to teach the objective. State both within-sample and between-sample assumptions,
and warn that normality does not imply independence across managers. Update the
numerical check and the evidence; agreement with `scipy.stats.f` only checks the
arithmetic, not the suitability of that distribution. A small, fixed-seed
equal-variance correlated-normal counterexample can show the failure of the
ordinary F calibration without adding a general testing framework.

**P2 — Include the empirical dependency group in the jet-fuel run command.**
`notes/artefacts/commodity-hedging/jet_fuel_hedge_test.py:8` says
`uv run python`, but its call to `backwardation_test.series()` imports `xlrd`.
The lock's default runtime/dev closure excludes `xlrd`; it belongs to the
optional `empirical` group. The current environment contains it, which masks
this defect. A clean `make sync` setup cannot run the documented command with
the required spreadsheets. Change it to `uv run --group empirical python ...`
and inspect the remaining current run instructions for transitive spreadsheet
dependencies. Preserve archived `superseded-*` scripts and their historical
commands. This documentation correction does not require rerunning the research
or moving spreadsheet libraries into the default environment.

**CI scope, not an unconditional defect.** The label-only trigger is deliberate.
The private guide checkout needs `GUIDE_READ_TOKEN`, and [GitHub withholds
repository secrets on fork-triggered pull requests](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/write-workflows/choose-what-workflows-do/use-secrets).
The current workflow consequently cannot validate such PRs through that secret.
Document the same-repository/trusted-branch limitation; supporting fork PRs is a
separate decision. Do not change to a privileged trigger to execute untrusted PR
code. No remote CI run or secret configuration was verified by this audit.

