## 17. Correction pass on PR #22 (executed 2026-10-18)

A fixes-only pass on draft PR #22 at `907e84b`. No objective was added and no
coverage status changed.

### 17.1 Content corrections

1. **Constrained frontier** (`meth:efficient-frontier`). The claim that
   long-only or inequality-constrained solutions necessarily have zero weights
   was wrong and has been removed. The entry now says:
   - An unconstrained optimum that already satisfies the added limits remains
     optimal.
   - Binding limits give boundary solutions and move the frontier right.
   - Each active set has its own closed form, so no single unrestricted formula
     covers changes of the active set.

   The existing example illustrates both cases. At 5 and 6 percent every weight
   is strictly positive and the long-only optimum is the same portfolio; at
   7.5 percent the long-only optimum has a zero bond weight (s.d. 15.49 against
   15.25). One focused assertion in `portfolio-choice-checks.py` checks this.
2. **Historical premium** (`meth:required-return-on-equity`, plus one sentence in
   `def:asset-class-characteristics`). Adding a historical premium to the current
   10-year yield is no longer called a mismatch: it is a legitimate CAPM estimate.
   - The entry keeps the dated 10.12 percent implied-premium example. It now
     gives 13.38 percent with the arithmetic and 11.60 percent with the
     geometric (5.48 percent) 1928-2025 premium over 10-year bonds.
   - It states the assumption that past premia inform the expected premium, and
     the estimation error: a two-standard-error band gives 8.54 to 18.21 percent.
   - It states that currency, horizon and the risk-free benchmark must match.
   - It separates these issues from mechanically pairing an implied premium with
     a rate other than its own (4.20 belongs with 5.29).
   - Source: Damodaran's corporate finance project notes (new key
     `corporate-finance-project-notes`, sec. V.a-b, read 2026-10-18). They cover
     the market-neutral implied premium against the historical premium for those
     who believe in reversion to averages, the 10-year rate in the analysis
     currency, and no historical premium for non-US firms.
   - Fama and French's 2.4-point standard error is now attributed to the premium
     over Treasury bills. One focused assertion was added to
     `asset-pricing-checks.py`.

### 17.2 Remote CI on merge only

`.github/workflows/ci.yml` now triggers on `pull_request` activity type
`closed`, filtered to base branch `main`. The job runs only if
`github.event.pull_request.merged == true`, and checks out
`github.event.pull_request.merge_commit_sha`, the merged revision on `main`.
- Commits, branch pushes, opening, updating and labelling a pull request
  trigger nothing.
- Closing an unmerged pull request records a run whose job is skipped.
- Permissions stay `contents: read`, there is no `pull_request_target`, and the
  validation steps are unchanged.
- The README describes the new trigger; local checks and commit hooks remain
  separate.

The `ready-to-merge` label still exists, with its old description; it no
longer triggers anything. Repository settings were not changed.

Validation was local only:
- `check-yaml` passed.
- A parse-and-simulate script confirmed the trigger, filters, permissions,
  checkout ref and step. Of seven event cases (push, opened, synchronize,
  labeled, closed unmerged, merged into another base, merged into main), only a
  merge into main runs the job.
- actionlint is not installed.

Live execution on a real merge event is unverified until the next merge.
Remote CI was not triggered in this pass.

### 17.3 Reconciliation of the earlier CI statement

Section 16 said remote CI was pending. Run 39119458178 on `907e84b`, triggered
by the `ready-to-merge` label under the previous workflow, passed
`make source-check`. It does not validate the commits of this pass.

### 17.4 Standing batch preference

Future content-expansion prompts should target **45 newly completed canonical
learning objectives per prompt** (raised from 30 on 2026-10-19; Section 19.2).
Fixes-only prompts are exempt.
- Count only previously uncovered objective IDs that move to `covered`. Entries,
  outline mappings and corrections to covered objectives do not count.
- The source-review (Section 3.5), teaching-depth, worked-example and
  mapping-review requirements are unchanged.
- If fewer than 45 objectives can be completed honestly, report the shortfall
  and its reasons rather than promoting unsupported coverage.

History: the batches of Sections 18 and 19 were run under the earlier
30-objective preference, and their records keep that figure.

### 17.5 Validation and limits

See the PR #22 description and `notes/source-review-records.md`, *Correction
pass on PR #22*, for the local check results. Coverage counts are unchanged:
41 of 424 objectives; 50 Level I and 6 Level II items; 974 of 1,030 unreviewed.

The five untracked `* 2.md` duplicates in `notes/readings/` were present at the
start of the batch session but were absent when this pass began; this pass did
not remove them and did not recreate them.

